  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSlow moving H5 upper low continues to move to the east and \nsoutheast across southern Manitoba this afternoon. Scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will be possible along the \nInternational Border and areas south to about Highway 2. A few \nstronger storms could form this afternoon in northwest \nMinnesota. Heading into Wednesday, showers and storms end during\nthe early morning, with dry weather the remainder of the day. \nTemperatures will be very similar each day through the weekend, \nwith widespread 70s to low 80s for high temps each afternoon. \nOur next chance for precipitation will be late Friday through \nthe weekend as another H5 shortwave moves slowly across the \nNorthern Plains.\n\n...Isolated Severe Thunderstorms This Evening...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are moving across northwest Minnesota\nthis afternoon, most being very light. This is in response to \nan upper low and H5 trough extending into northwest Minnesota, \nin the presence of modest instability and peak daytime heating. \nAt this time, the best forcing remains east of the area; \nhowever, there is just enough forcing, along small lobes of \nhigher vorticity that are moving along the flow to the south of \nthe main H5 low, to allow a mention of isolated strong to severe\nstorms this afternoon. Best chances will extend southward along\nand east of a line from Lake of the Woods to about Wadena, MN. \nThe primary concern will be hail up to 1 inch in diameter, with \na lower chance for damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph.",
